course: dictionary
lesson: 9627
title: Stuck: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one you need. «When you are stuck, calling a grown-up is the smart thing to do.» Here stuck means you cannot go on with something because it is too hard.
explain: «She stuck the stamp on the card.» Here stuck is the past form of stick, so it means she pressed the stamp on so it stayed. Stuck can also describe a state: «stuck in the mud» or «stuck in a tree» means not able to move.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does «When you are not able to go on, calling a grown-up is the smart thing to do» still make sense? Yes, so stuck means not able to go on. The words in, on and with after stuck are clues too.
fact: Stuck is the past form of stick: stick, stuck.
fact: «Stuck» can mean not able to move, as in «stuck in the mud».
fact: «Stuck» can mean not able to go on with something because it is too hard, as in «stuck on a question».
fact: «Stuck with something» means you have to keep it even if you do not want it.
fact: Asking for help when stuck is clever, not weak, and it often makes homework faster.
exercise: «She stuck the stamp on the card.» What does stuck mean here? | Not able to move | Not able to go on because it is too hard | Having to keep something you do not want | *The past form of stick, meaning she pressed the stamp on so it stayed
exercise: «The car was stuck in traffic for an hour.» What does stuck mean? | *Not able to move | Very angry | Finished early | Glued firmly on
exercise: Ben says, «I'm stuck on this sum.» What does he mean? | He is angry with the sum | He has finished the sum | *He cannot go on with the sum because it is too hard | He stuck the sum to the wall
test: «Asking for help when stuck is clever, not weak.» In this sentence, what does stuck mean? | Very hungry | Glued to something | *Not able to go on with a task because it is too hard | Lost in a forest
test: Which sentence uses stuck as the past form of stick? | The dog was stuck in the mud | Max is stuck on a hard question | *Ella stuck a sticker on her book | Sam was stuck with a boring job
exam: «Stuck in the lift, Mia waited for help.» What does stuck mean here? | *Not able to move | Ready to go | Pressed tightly against the wall | Full of fun
